wsi/metal: IOSurface swapchains: at most two Core Animation commits per refresh, kernel calls only for shown frames

Minecraft 26.3 at ~950 fps (IMMEDIATE) spent 8.7% of its render thread in vkQueuePresentKHR, 6.4% of it in
CA::Transaction::commit, plus the IOSurface write-fence ioctls of every present and the acquire's GPU-idle wait.
The window server composites once per display refresh, so frames beyond that are never shown.

- IMMEDIATE still shows a frame at present when the last one went out at least half a refresh ago; a frame
  presented sooner is held, and the refresh thread shows the newest held one (latest-wins, without waiting for its
  rendering: implicit sync). At most two commits per refresh instead of one per frame.
- IMMEDIATE never makes acquire wait for that: an acquire that finds no free image shows the held frame at once, and
  IMMEDIATE swapchains, like MAILBOX ones, get at least four images (the shown one, the one the window server is
  letting go of, the held one, one to render into; more images than minImageCount is allowed). Images the window
  server still holds are polled every 50 us instead of 200 us.
- A frame's write fence is attached to its IOSurface (and the VRAM copy marked newer, the VRAM tag bumped) when it is
  shown, not when it is presented: a frame replaced before it was shown costs no kernel calls.
- Acquire waits for the window server's GPU reads only of an image that was shown since the application last had it.

wsitest 1280x720, IMMEDIATE, 3 images asked: 13914 fps, acquire 0.002 ms, 466 shown/s (wsi2: 4591 fps, acquire
0.100 ms, every frame committed); 2560x1302: 5726 fps (wsi2: 4779). MAILBOX 6313 fps; all VERIFY PASS.

